.TH GITCHECK 1 "October 2026" "gitcheck 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gitcheck \- report on, and optionally tidy up, every git repository in a directory

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gitcheck
[\-u] [\-c] [\-f] [\-N] [\-b] [\-l] [\-s] [\-i]
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B gitcheck
\-V | \-h

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gitcheck
looks at each immediate subdirectory of
.I dir
(default: the current directory) and, for every one that is a git repository, prints a short report.
Subdirectories that are not repositories are flagged ("Not a Git repository"), except ones whose names
match, in any case,
.BR tmp ,
.BR temp ,
.BR bak ,
.BR backup ,
.BR backups ,
.BR old ,
.BR trash ,
.BR cache ,
.BR *.bak ,
.B *.old
or
.BR *\(ti ,
and ones listed in the ignore file (see
.BR FILES ).
These names only hide directories that are not repositories; a repository is always reported.
It is meant to be run over a folder of
clones \- such as
.I \(ti/github/USER
\- to see at a glance what still needs committing or pushing.

Every report starts with whether the repository has a GitHub remote. If the GitHub CLI
.BR gh (1)
is installed and signed in,
.B gitcheck
also says whether the repository the remote points to
.RI ( owner/name
taken from the URL, whatever the local directory is called) exists, looking it up with
.B gh repo view
if it is not among the signed-in account's own repositories
("Yes (exists on GitHub)" or "Yes (but NOT on GitHub)"); without
.B gh
it just says "Yes". The options choose what else is reported; at least one must be given.
.B gh
is also needed to list repositories that are on GitHub but not cloned
.RB ( \-u ).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
After the repositories, list the signed-in account's
GitHub repositories that are not cloned in
.I dir
(no directory of that name, and no repository whose remote points to it, whatever its directory is called).
Ones marked "don't ask again" by
.B \-i
are shown with
.BR "(don't clone)" .
Needs
.BR gh .
With
.BR \-i ,
offers to clone them.
.TP
.B \-c
Uncommitted changes, unpulled commits and unpushed commits.
.RS
.PP
If the working tree has changes, prints the branch they are on (or "detached at
.IR commit ")"
and two counts:
.B tracked files changed
(modified, staged, deleted or renamed) and
.B untracked files
(new files that are not ignored). A new directory counts as one untracked entry, as in
.BR "git status" .
.PP
Then
.B unpulled commits:
each local branch whose upstream has commits the branch does not, with their number and the upstream
they are on. Potential conflicts are flagged with
.B \[u26A0]
(in bold red on a terminal):
.IP \(bu 2
the branch has also diverged (it has commits of its own): the merge is tried in memory with
.B git merge-tree
(nothing is changed), and the files that would conflict are listed, or it says the branches merge
cleanly;
.IP \(bu 2
on the current branch, uncommitted changes (or untracked files) touch files the upstream also
changed, so a pull would stop or conflict; those files are listed.
.PP
Then
.B unpushed commits:
each local branch with commits that are not on its upstream, with the number of such
commits. A branch with no upstream (or whose upstream was deleted) is compared with the branch of
the same name on the default remote, if there is one, and otherwise counts the commits found on no
remote-tracking branch at all. A branch is never judged by an unrelated remote branch that happens to
contain its commits.
Branches marked "don't ask again" by
.B \-i
are shown with
.BR "(local only)" ,
and do not count: if every branch with unpushed commits is marked, the line reads
.BR "Unpushed commits: No (only on local-only branches)" .
A repository marked local-only (see
.B Local-only repository
under
.BR "INTERACTIVE MODE" )
is not checked for unpushed commits at all, and says so.
.PP
Each repository is first fetched from all its remotes (with
.BR \-\-prune ,
so branches deleted on the remote are forgotten), so that unpulled and unpushed commits are judged
against the remotes as they are now. If a fetch fails, that is reported and the last successful fetch
is used. See
.BR \-N .
.RE
.TP
.B \-f
Fetch from all remotes first, then say whether the current branch is up to date with, behind, ahead
of, or diverged from its upstream.
.TP
.B \-N
Do not fetch (with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-f ):
work from the remotes as of the last fetch. Faster and works offline, but changes made on the remote
since are not seen.
.TP
.B \-b
List all local and remote-tracking branches.
.TP
.B \-l
List files over 1 MB (10^6 bytes) in the
.B HEAD
commit.
.TP
.B \-s
Summary: number of commits on all branches, number of local branches, and the time of the last
commit.
.TP
.B \-i
Interactive; must be combined with
.B \-c
and/or
.BR \-u .
With
.BR \-c ,
after each part of that report, asks what to do about it: add or ignore untracked files, commit,
pull, set upstreams and push. With
.BR \-u ,
offers to clone the repositories that are not cloned here. Either way, offers to make each flagged
subdirectory that is not a repository a new one, or to stop flagging it. See
.B INTERACTIVE MODE
below.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.

.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
Prompts are read from the terminal
.RI ( /dev/tty ),
not from standard input. Answers are single letters, in either case; anything else asks again.
.B q
at any prompt exits
.B gitcheck
at once, leaving everything done so far in place. The "Not a repository" prompt comes with any
.BR \-i ,
the "Clean-up pending" prompt and the six after the next one with
.BR \-c ,
and the last with
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B Clean-up pending
With
.BR \-c ,
if the directory being checked holds a non-empty
.B tmp-branches.txt
(one "repo branch" per line; blank lines and # comments are ignored) and an executable
.BR clean-up-branches.sh ,
the number of listed branches is reported before any repository is processed. Without
.B \-i
that is all. With it, asked once:
.B y
runs the script (in that directory) now, so branches that are done are removed instead of being
offered a push of their own;
.B d
runs it with
.B \-n
(a dry run) and asks again;
.B n
goes on without it;
.B q
exits.
.TP
.B Not a repository
For each flagged subdirectory that is not a repository:
.B g
makes it a new repository with
.BR "git init" ,
then asks about its files and an initial commit exactly as for an existing repository (the
"Untracked files and folders" and "Commit" prompts below). Then, if
.B gh
is signed in and the account has no repository of that name, offers to create one
.RB ( p rivate
or
.RB p u blic)
with
.BR "gh repo create" ,
set it as
.B origin
and push the initial commit.
.B i
adds its full path to the ignore file (see
.BR FILES ),
so it is never flagged again;
.B s
leaves it flagged.
.TP
.B Untracked files and folders
One prompt for each:
.B a
stages it
.RB ( "git add" ),
.B i
appends it to the repository's top-level
.I .gitignore
and stages that file,
.B s
leaves it alone. The ignore entry is anchored to the repository root (a leading
.BR / )
and has
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.B [
and
.B ]
escaped, so it matches only that one path.
.TP
.B Commit
Shows the tracked changes (the first 20, then a count of the rest) and offers to commit
.B a
all tracked changes
.RB ( "git add \-u" ,
plus anything staged above),
.B s
only what is already staged (offered only when something is), or
.B n
nothing. It then asks for a one-line commit message; an empty message cancels the commit and leaves
the changes staged. Commit hooks run as usual.
.TP
.B Pull
For each branch with unpulled commits, only where it is safe:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If the branch has no commits of its own, offers a fast-forward
.RB ( y / n ):
.B git merge \-\-ff\-only
on the current branch, or an update of the branch ref for one that is not checked out.
.IP \(bu 2
If it has diverged, is the current branch and
.B git merge-tree
says the merge is clean, offers
.B m
to merge
.RB ( "git merge \-\-no\-edit" ),
.B r
to rebase onto the upstream
.RB ( "git rebase \-\-autostash" )
or
.B n
neither. If either fails anyway it is aborted, leaving the branch as it was.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise it says why it is not pulling and moves on: uncommitted changes touch files the upstream
changed (commit or stash them first), the merge would conflict (resolve it by hand), or the diverged
branch is not checked out.
.RE
.IP
Pulls are offered before pushes, so a branch that is both behind and ahead can be merged and then
pushed in the same run.
.TP
.B Set upstream
For each branch with unpushed commits and no upstream:
.B y
sets its upstream to the branch of the same name on
.B origin
(or the first remote if there is no
.BR origin ),
without pushing;
.B n
marks the branch local-only and never asks about it again;
.B s
skips it for this run only. Branches with no remote at all are reported and skipped.
.IP
The main branch is what
.B origin/HEAD
points to, else
.B main
or
.BR master .
A branch whose commits are all in it already is said to be so, and
.B d
deletes the branch (nothing is lost: every commit stays reachable from the main branch).
For a branch with commits that are not in the main branch,
.B m
merges it there: a fast-forward when that is all it takes (also when the main branch is not checked out),
otherwise a real merge, but only while the main branch is checked out; a merge that conflicts is aborted
and leaves everything as it was. After a merge it asks whether to delete the branch. A merged or deleted
branch needs no upstream or push of its own: its commits go with the main branch, which is offered for
pushing in its turn.
.TP
.B Local-only repository
For a repository with no remote at all, where unpushed commits have nowhere to go (and not every
unpushed branch is already marked "local only"), asked once per repository:
.B y
marks the whole repository local-only by setting
.B gitcheck.localOnly
to true in its own git config, after which its unpushed commits are no longer checked or flagged;
.B s
skips it for this run only. The mark can be set or removed by hand, also for a repository that has
a remote:
.B git config \-\-bool gitcheck.localOnly true
and
.BR "git config \-\-unset gitcheck.localOnly" .
.TP
.B Push
For each branch with unpushed commits and an upstream (including one just set):
.B y
pushes the branch to its upstream,
.B n
doesn't. Pull comes first: a branch that is still behind its upstream (because the pull above was
declined or was not safe) is not offered a push, and the reason is given.
.PP
A branch checked out in another worktree is never pulled, merged into, or deleted by
.BR gitcheck :
git would refuse, so it says where the branch is checked out.
.TP
.B Clone
With
.BR \-u ,
for each GitHub repository not cloned in
.IR dir :
.B y
clones it there with
.BR "gh repo clone" ,
.B n
records it in
.I \(ti/.config/gitcheck/noclone
and never asks about it again,
.B s
skips it for this run only.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B branch.\fIname\fB.gitcheckNoUpstream
Set to
.B true
in a repository's
.I .git/config
when you answer
.B n
to setting an upstream for branch
.IR name .
To be asked again:
.RS
.nf
git config \-\-unset branch.\fIname\fR.gitcheckNoUpstream
.fi
.RE
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gitcheck/noclone
(default
.IR \(ti/.config/gitcheck/noclone )
GitHub repositories, one
.I owner/name
per line, that you answered
.B n
to cloning. Delete a line to be asked again.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gitcheck/ignore
(default
.IR \(ti/.config/gitcheck/ignore )
Subdirectories that are not repositories and should not be flagged, in addition to the built-in
names. One per line: a pattern such as
.B scratch*
matches a directory name in any
.IR dir ;
one that starts with
.B /
matches a full path, such as
.BR /home/chris/github/WebAutomation .
Patterns are shell globs; blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are skipped. Answering
.B i
to a "Not a repository" prompt adds the directory's full path, with glob characters escaped. Delete a
line to have it flagged again.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GITCHECK_TTY
File to read interactive answers from instead of
.IR /dev/tty .
Used by the test suite.

.SH EXAMPLES
What needs committing or pushing in all my clones:
.RS
.B gitcheck \-c \(ti/github/chrispollitt
.RE

The same, and deal with it as I go, cloning any GitHub repositories I don't have yet:
.RS
.B gitcheck \-u \-i \(ti/github/chrispollitt
.RE

Fetch everything and show where each current branch stands, with a summary:
.RS
.B gitcheck \-f \-s
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Finished, or quit with
.B q
in interactive mode.
.TP
.B 1
The terminal could not be read in interactive mode (or ran out of answers, with
.BR GITCHECK_TTY ).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or
.I dir
could not be entered.

.SH NOTES
On Cygwin, a repository can show many "changed" tracked files that differ only in file mode. Answering
.B a
at the commit prompt commits those too; check with
.B git diff
first, or set
.B core.fileMode false
in that repository.

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-status (1),
.BR git-push (1),
.BR gitignore (5),
.BR gh (1)
